USING KEY is a novel approach to recursive common table expressions in SQL, extending the semantics of the classic WITH RECURSIVE clause to solve many of its issues. WITH RECURSIVE collects all intermediate results in the append-only union table, while USING KEY enables updating previous results in the recurring table, unlocking runtime, memory and syntactic advantages. Due to its novelty, there is a lack of queries actually utilizing USING KEY. Existing work lacks thorough code- and performance-level comparison between the two variants. This thesis presents four algorithms - A\*, Longest Common Subsequence, Needleman-Wunsch and Drunken Bishop - in both variants to compare their implementation, runtime and memory performance. The first three algorithms rely on accessing results from any previous iteration, which allows them to benefit from USING KEY across all metrics; Drunken Bishop reads only immediately preceding results, which makes the overhead of USING KEY outweigh its benefits.